WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Here we'll use a reported discount
bug to walk through an automation.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.140
I'll start with the GitHub issue trigger,
define the task and its destination,

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.400
then follow a run from the
incoming issue to its result.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.480
We'll inspect the proposed fix and
regression tests in the pull request.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.480
Finally, I'll return to the
run history and show where

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.960
to refine the instructions
or pause the workflow.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.060
I open automations and choose
create PR from GitHub issue.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.080
Our example is a discount boundary bug.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.960
An order reaches the minimum
quantity but the discount

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.660
doesn't apply until the
customer adds another item.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.460
I select the demo repository
and the issue opened event.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.960
That defines which GitHub
event starts the work.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.760
The connection status confirms
that the GitHub app is connected,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.600
the repository is installed
and the event is synced.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.700
GitHub can now send a
matching event to CodeRabbit.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.780
The trigger tells the
automation when to start.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.440
Next, I'll define the
task it should carry out.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.840
In the instructions, I describe
the work in plain language,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.880
investigate the reported problem, make
a focused fix and test the result.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.540
I ask the agent to check whether the bug
is already fixed before editing anything.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.180
If a change is needed,
the test should cover an

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.580
order below the minimum,
exactly at it and above it.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.460
I also specify the behavior to preserve,

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.120
including the discount
amount and expiration rule.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.780
For the destination, I select the
GitHub repository and request one

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.820
pull request with an explanation,
test results and a link to the issue.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.520
The pull request should
remain unmerged for review.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.880
Those instructions will be reused
for matching events so it's

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.180
worth being clear about the scope
and the evidence we want back.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.860
With the trigger, instructions
and destination saved,

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.100
I check that the automation is active.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.920
Here's the issue used for this run.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.580
Its title identifies the missing
discount at the exact minimum quantity.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.080
The description explains how
to reproduce it and gives

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.720
the expected behavior below,
at and above the boundary.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.300
The issue also asks the fix to preserve the

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.240
existing discount and checkout behavior.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.180
I return to automations and open activity.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.840
This run was started by that GitHub
issue using the instructions we saved.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.760
I can follow the agent's investigation
and read the results here.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.980
The completed run reports
that it opened a pull request

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.540
containing the threshold
fix and regression tests.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.500
I follow the result link to
inspect what it produced.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.980
On GitHub, I check the pull
request destination branch,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.620
changed files and link back to the issue.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.660
The code change includes the minimum
quantity in the discount comparison.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.040
I open the test to see how
the boundary is covered.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.360
There are cases below, at
and above the threshold,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.100
using two different minimum quantities.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.100
The tests also check that an expired
promotion doesn't give a discount.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.760
The recorded run reports
70 passed tests and 5

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.860
existing expected failures
for unrelated problems.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.760
GitHub's automated checks also passed.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.740
There is a separate review limitation here.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.360
CodeRabbit skipped its
pull request review because

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.760
the author is also the
configured reviewer account.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.440
The passing tests don't
stand in for that review.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.780
I leave the pull request
open for the team to inspect.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.180
The automation has prepared a
proposed fix and its evidence.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.880
The team still decides
whether to accept it.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.120
I return to the automation's
overview and open activity.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.400
The history shows when it ran,

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.280
what triggered the work
and the agent minutes used.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.160
Opening a run lets me read
its result in context.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.400
That's where I'd look if a
task reported a blocker or

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.400
delivered something different
from what I expected.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.920
I can return to the
configuration to refine the

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.400
instructions or destination
for future runs.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.560
If I want to stop processing new
events, I can pause the automation.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.780
We've defined the task once and followed an

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.840
incoming issue through to a proposed fix.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.700
The run history and
configuration give us a way

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.120
to improve that workflow
as we see the results.
